:title("Bash interpreter") ;
:description("Parses and executes practical Bash directly during the compile walk, without
generating code. Every value is a string (Bash's only data type); integer arithmetic converts on
demand. Commands return an integer exit status (0 = success, like the shell); `return` yields the
wrapper {ret: n} that the enclosing statement thunks pass on, `exit` the same wrapper marked
isExit (so it unwinds past callers but is caught by a subshell), and break / continue travel as
{brk: n} / {cont: n} through the same one protocol.

All 22 sections of tests/bash-test-full.sh pass, goja and -frozen byte-identical:
  - quoting: double, single, $'...' ANSI-C escapes, backslash escapes, line continuation
  - the whole ${...} family: ${v} ${#v} ${!v} ${v:-d} ${v-d} ${v:=d} ${v:+a} ${v:?m}
    ${v:off:len} ${v#p} ${v##p} ${v%p} ${v%%p} ${v/p/r} ${v//p/r} ${v/#p/r} ${v/%p/r}
    ${v^} ${v^^} ${v,} ${v,,} and every subscripted form of them
  - command substitution $(...) and `...`, nested; v=$(cmd) leaves cmd's status in $?
  - arithmetic $(( )), (( )) and let, 64-bit and wrapping like bash's intmax_t: the full
    operator set (** * / % + - << >> comparisons
    == != & ^ | && || ?: the assignment forms ++ -- and the comma) with hexadecimal, octal
    and BASE#DIGITS literals
  - if/elif/else, while, until, for..in, for NAME (the positional parameters), for (( ;; )),
    case with glob patterns and the ;; ;& ;;& terminators, break/continue with a level
  - [[ ]] conditionals: glob == and != (a quoted pattern is literal), =~ with BASH_REMATCH
    through the shared engine in lib/regex.js, string and integer comparisons, ! && || and
    parentheses; and the test builtin as a real expression, with ! -a -o and groups
  - functions in both spellings; local with REAL dynamic scoping, positional parameters,
    $# $* $@ $? $FUNCNAME, shift and set --
  - indexed and associative arrays: a=(x y z) a[i]=v a+=(w) ${a[i]} ${a[@]} ${a[*]} ${!a[*]}
    ${#a[@]} ${a[*]:o:n}, unset a[i], declare -a/-A/-i/-u/-l, and read -a
  - word splitting on IFS, brace expansion (lists, ranges and cross products) and tilde
  - pipelines with |, ! to negate one, PIPESTATUS, && and || lists; each stage runs in a
    subshell (no lastpipe) with its stdout captured and handed to the next stage as
    standard input, which read consumes
  - subshells ( LIST ) with variable, positional-parameter, option and exit isolation, and
    brace groups { LIST; } that run in the current shell
  - redirections > >> < >& <& &> >| , here-strings <<< and here-documents << and <<-
  - builtins: echo (-n -e), printf (with -v), read, test/[, local/declare/typeset/export/
    readonly, unset, shift, set (-e -u -o pipefail and --), trap ... EXIT, eval, break,
    continue, return, exit, true/false/:

Two things have no counterpart behind a grammar run and are MODELLED rather than faked. There
is no filesystem, so the file predicates (-e -f -d ...) answer from a small model of the
standard paths (/dev/null and friends exist, / and /tmp and a few others are directories,
everything else is absent), and an output redirection discards the output instead of writing a
file while an input redirection other than a here-document is end of file at once. There is no
process environment or process id, so HOME, PWD and IFS start at fixed documented values and
$$ / $! are constants. Standard error is a real second CHANNEL: which channel a redirection
routes a command to is modelled exactly, and this half writes channel 1 to the host's standard
error (line buffered - a final partial line gains a newline, the one byte this cannot match).

Still out of scope: external commands of every kind, job control, process substitution,
globbing against a real directory, and getopts.") ;


// --- main rules

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;
:include("lib/bash-splice.abnf") ;

// A whole script: statements separated by ; or newlines (blank / comment lines collapse).
// The production tag runs all top level statement thunks and exits with the final status.
// A top-level separator run that holds a newline also ENDS A LINE ({nl}): an expansion
// error abandons the rest of its line, as bash abandons the command line it read.
Program     <~~ runProgram(takeAll()) ~~>
            = BsSplice OptSep [ Statement { TopSep Statement } TopOptSep ] ;
TopSep      = TopAmpSep | TopSepRun ;
TopOptSep   = [ TopSep ] ;
TopAmpSep   <~~ push({amp: 1}); pushLineEnd(up.in) ~~> = AmpTok { NotCaseTerm SepTok } ;
TopSepRun   <~~ pushLineEnd(up.in) ~~> = NotCaseTerm SepTok { NotCaseTerm SepTok } ;

// Statement separators: one or more ; or newlines. Leading whitespace (incl. #comments) is
// skipped before each separator token, so blank and comment-only lines vanish here.
// NotCaseTerm is here, not only in CaseSep: ";;" / ";&" / ";;&" are the CASE-CLAUSE
// terminators and nothing else. `echo 1 ;; echo 2` and `echo a;&` are syntax errors in real
// bash, so a run of separators must never swallow one as "two semicolons".
OptSep      = [ Sep ] ;
Sep         = NotCaseTerm SepTok { NotCaseTerm SepTok } ;
SepTok      = ";" | "\n" ;

// `LIST &` runs the list ASYNCHRONOUSLY, and "&" is a list separator in its own right, so
// `a & b`, `a &` and `a &<newline>b` are all one statement list. Exactly one "&" may open a
// separator run (`a & & b` is a syntax error in bash too), and NotAmp keeps "&&" out of it.
// The {amp} marker lands in the enclosing statement list, where linkAsync() turns it into a
// wrapper around the statement it followed.
ListSep     = AmpSep | Sep ;
ListOptSep  = [ ListSep ] ;
AmpSep      <~~ push({amp: 1}) ~~> = AmpTok { NotCaseTerm SepTok } ;
AmpTok      = "&" NotAmp ;

Statement   = AndOrList ;

// A brace / keyword delimited body: at least one statement. The trailing OptSep swallows the
// final ; or newline before the closing keyword (fi / done / }), and a reserved word in
// command position (guarded in SimpleCommand) stops the list so that keyword can match.
CompoundList <~~ push(makeSeq(linkAsync(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = OptSep Statement { ListSep Statement } ListOptSep ;
// An eval or trap string, its continuations joined (see compileSnippet).
Spliced     <~~ push(up.in) ~~> = BsSplice :whitespace() { AnyCh | "\n" } ;
// An eval or trap string runs line by line like a script (see makeLines).
Snippet     <~~ push(makeLines(splitLines(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = OptSep [ Statement { TopSep Statement } TopOptSep ] ;


// --- compound statements

// if LIST; then LIST; [elif LIST; then LIST;]* [else LIST;] fi
IfStmt      <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = IfClause { ElifClause } [ ElseClause ] KwFi ;
IfClause    <~~ push(mkCond(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf CompoundList KwThen CompoundList ;
ElifClause  <~~ push(mkCond(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwElif CompoundList KwThen CompoundList ;
ElseClause  <~~ push(mkElse(pop())) ~~>
            = KwElse CompoundList ;

// case WORD in [(] PAT [| PAT]... ) LIST ;; ... esac
// EVERY WORD HERE IS AN NsWord, and so is every operand of [[ ]] below. bash does NOT
// field-split the subject of a `case`, its patterns, or either side of a [[ ]] test -
// they are the same "unquoted but unsplit" context an assignment's right hand side is.
// The body of a clause is an ordinary statement list, except that its separator run must not
// swallow the leading ";" of the ";;" that ends the clause - which is what NotCaseTerm guards.
CaseStmt    <~~ push(makeCase(takeAll(), lineStr(up.start))) ~~>
            = KwCase NsWord KwIn CaseSepOpt { CaseClause } KwEsac ;
CaseClause  <~~ push(mkCaseClause(takeAll())) ~~>
            = CaseOpen CasePats ")" CaseBody [ CaseTerm ] CaseSepOpt ;
// Without its "(", a clause cannot begin with the reserved word `esac`: that word ends the
// case, so `$(case $x in a) echo q;; esac)` closes the $( ) with its ")", and bash rejects
// `case x in esac) ...` while `(esac)` is a pattern.
CaseOpen    = "(" | NotEsac ;
CasePats    <~~ push({pats: takeAll()}) ~~> = CasePat { "|" CasePat } ;
// A case PATTERN is a word whose QUOTED parts are literal (`'*')` matches only "*"): the
// quoting-aware segments of [[ == ]]'s right hand side (PatSq, PatDq, PatAnsi, PatEsc).
CasePat     <~~ badClaim(up.start, up.pos, true); push(mkNsWord(takeAll())) ~~> = CaseFirst :whitespace() { CaseSeg } ;
CaseFirst   = Tilde | CaseSeg ;
CaseSeg     = PatSq | PatDq | PatAnsi | UqExp | PatEsc | BraceLit | ExtGlob | EgPfxLit | BareSeg ;
CaseBody    <~~ push(makeSeq(linkAsync(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = CaseSepOpt [ Statement { ListSep Statement } ListOptSep ] ;
CaseSep     = NotCaseTerm SepTok { NotCaseTerm SepTok } ;
CaseSepOpt  = [ CaseSep ] ;
CaseTerm    = CaseNl CaseTermTok ;
CaseNl      = { "\n" } ;
CaseTermTok <~~ push({cterm: up.in}) ~~> = ";;&" | ";;" | ";&" ;

// while LIST; do LIST; done   /   until LIST; do LIST; done (the negated condition)
WhileStmt   <~~ push(makeWhile(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwWhile CompoundList KwDo CompoundList KwDone ;
UntilStmt   <~~ push(makeUntil(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwUntil CompoundList KwDo CompoundList KwDone ;

// for (( init; cond; step )); do LIST; done
// Each part is arithmetic text that, like (( )), is expanded FIRST when it holds an
// expansion (e='1 + 2'; `i < $e * 2` is i < 5), and may be empty: an empty condition is 1.
CForStmt    <~~ push(makeCFor(takeAll(), lineStr(up.start))) ~~>
            = KwFor "((" :whitespace() CfPart ";" CfCond ";" CfPart "))" :whitespace(Whitespace) OptSep LoopBody ;
CfPart      = CfDyn | CfSt | CfRaw | CfZero ;
CfCond      = CfDyn | CfSt | CfRaw | CfOne ;
CfDyn       <~~ arSqClaim(up.start, up.pos); badClaim(arPastWs(up.start), up.pos, true); push(mkArDyn(pop())) ~~> = CfHasExp CfDynWord ;
CfDynWord   <~~ push(mkNsWord(takeAll())) ~~> = CfDynSeg { CfDynSeg } ;
CfDynSeg    = DqSeg | ExpSeg | ArDynParen | ArDynSub | CfDynBare | ArDynLb ;
CfDynBare   <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"()[$`\";" ;
CfSt        <~~ arClaim(up.start, up.pos); push(pop()) ~~> = WsNL :whitespace(Whitespace) ArList :whitespace() WsNL CfEnd ;
// A part the parser does not take (`b[]=0`) is read by the run-time evaluator, as (( )) is.
CfRaw       <~~ arSqClaim(up.start, up.pos); badClaim(arPastWs(up.start), up.pos, true); push(mkArDyn(pop())) ~~> = WsNL !";" !"))" CfDynWord ;
CfZero      <~~ push(mkNum("0")) ~~> = WsNL ;
CfOne       <~~ push(mkNum("1")) ~~> = WsNL ;

// for NAME in WORD...; do LIST; done   /   for NAME; do LIST; done (the positional parameters)
// Each word is one iteration value (no re-splitting).
ForStmt     <~~ push(makeFor(takeAll(), lineStr(up.start))) ~~>
            = KwFor ForName [ ForIn ] Sep LoopBody ;
// A for loop's body is do LIST done or, as bash also takes it, { LIST; }.
LoopBody    = ( KwDo CompoundList KwDone ) | ( "{" BraceKwEnd CompoundList "}" ) ;
ForIn       = KwIn ForWords ;
ForName     <~~ push({fname: up.in}) ~~>
            = Name ;
ForWords    <~~ push({words: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = { CWord } ;

// NAME() { LIST; }, and the `function NAME [()]  { LIST; }` keyword form.
FuncDef     <~~ push(makeFuncDef(takeAll(), arSrc(up.start, up.pos))) ~~>
            = FuncDefHead OptSep FuncBody [ RedirList ] ;
// The redirections after the body belong to it: they apply at every call (`f() { echo a; }
// >/dev/null; f` prints nothing).
// A function body may be ANY compound command; { LIST; } and ( LIST ) are the two that
// appear in the wild ( `f() ( cd /tmp; pwd )` runs the body in a subshell ).
FuncBody    = FuncBraceBody | Subshell | IfStmt | CForStmt | ForStmt | WhileStmt | UntilStmt | CaseStmt | ArithCmd | CondCmd ;
FuncBraceBody <~~ push(makeGroup(pop())) ~~> = "{" BraceKwEnd CompoundList "}" ;
FuncDefHead = ( KwFunction FuncName [ "(" ")" ] ) | ( FuncName "(" ")" ) ;
FuncName    = FuncNameTok :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A bash function name is a WORD, not an identifier. The corpus is full of real ones:
//     show-len() { ... }   py-repr() { ... }   ble/foo() { ... }   test-hyphen() { ... }
// so anything that is not a blank, a separator, a metacharacter or a quote is allowed.
FuncNameTok <~~ push({fname: up.in}) ~~>
            = !@+" \t\n\r;&|<>(){}$'\"\\`=" ;

Name        = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) :whitespace() { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;


// --- simple commands, && / || lists and assignments

// cmd1 && cmd2 || cmd3 : left to right, short circuit on the running exit status.
AndOrOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~>
            = "&&" | "||" ;

// A pipeline is what && / || and ! actually operate on. Each stage runs with its stdout
// captured and handed to the next stage as its standard input, and in a subshell.
AndOrList   <~~ push(makeAndOr(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Pipeline { AndOrOp Linebreak Pipeline } ;
Pipeline    <~~ push(makePipeline(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ BangOp ] Command { PipeOp Linebreak Command } ;
// Newlines may follow && || and | (and stand around [[ ]] terms, see CondNot).
Linebreak   = { "\n" } ;
BangOp      <~~ push({bang: 1}) ~~> = "!" KwEnd ;
PipeOp      = "|" NotBar ;

// A compound command is an ordinary Command, so it can be a pipeline stage
// (`echo x | while read l; do ...; done`) and can carry redirections that apply to the
// whole thing (`while read l; do ...; done < file`, `for x in ...; done <<EOF`). Its
// {cpd} marker tells makePipeline that, as a stage, it starts outside every loop.
Command     = CompoundCmd | LetCmd | DeclCommand | PrefixDecl | PrefixCommand | Assignment | RedirCommand | SimpleCommand ;
CompoundCmd <~~ push({cpd: 1}) ~~> = CompoundRedir | Subshell | BraceGroup ;
CompoundRedir <~~ push(mkRedirected(takeAll())) ~~> = CompoundCore [ RedirList ] ;
// "((" must be tried before "(" or an arithmetic command parses as nested subshells, which
// is why CompoundRedir comes before Subshell rather than after it.
CompoundCore = ArithCmd | CondCmd | FlowCmd ;
// The compound commands set -e does not look at as a whole (eeExempt): what failed in them was
// answered there.
FlowCmd     <~~ push(eeExempt(pop())) ~~> = IfStmt | CForStmt | ForStmt | WhileStmt | UntilStmt | CaseStmt | FuncDef ;

// NAME=VALUE... COMMAND ARGS : the assignments apply only for the duration of the command.
// Tried before a bare Assignment, which is the same shape without a command word after it.
// Redirections may stand before and among the assignments (`>/dev/null x=5 cmd`).
PrefixCommand <~~ push(makePrefixCmd(takeAll(), "" + lnPop())) ~~>
            = { RedirItem } AsgPrefix1 { AsgPrefix | RedirItem } NotIoRedir NotReserved CWord { CmdSuffix } ;
AsgPrefix1  <~~ lnPush(up.pos) ~~> = AsgPrefix ;
// A subscripted one (`a[1]=Q cmd`) parses too: bash refuses it when it runs (mkPrefixAsg).
AsgPrefix   <~~ push(mkPrefixAsg(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AsgIdent <~~ push({aname: up.in}) ~~> :whitespace() [ AsgIdx ] AsgPOp :whitespace() [ NsWord ] ;
AsgPOp      <~~ push({pop: up.in}) ~~> = "+=" | AsgEq ;
AsgEq       = "=" ;
// NAME=VALUE... before a declaration builtin (makePrefixDecl has the rules).
PrefixDecl  <~~ push(makePrefixDecl(takeAll(), "" + lnPop())) ~~>
            = AsgPrefix1 { AsgPrefix } DeclKw { DeclFlag } { DeclItem } [ RedirList ] ;

// ( LIST ) runs in a copy of the shell: variable changes and `exit` stay inside.
Subshell    <~~ push(makeSubshellR(takeAll())) ~~> = "(" CompoundList ")" [ RedirList ] ;
// { LIST; } is a plain grouping: it runs in the CURRENT shell. "{" only opens a group when
// it is a WORD OF ITS OWN - `{ls; }` is the word "{ls" followed by a stray "}", which real
// bash refuses ("syntax error near unexpected token `}'"), so BraceKwEnd demands the blank.
BraceGroup  <~~ push(eeExempt(makeGroupR(takeAll()))) ~~> = "{" BraceKwEnd CompoundList "}" [ RedirList ] ;
// (( expr )) as a command: status 0 when the value is non-zero.
// (( EXPR )): a text holding an expansion is expanded first and then evaluated, exactly as
// $(( EXPR )) is (see ArithExp).
ArithCmd    = ArithCmdDyn | ArithCmdSt | ArithCmdRaw ;
ArithCmdSt  <~~ arClaim(up.start + 2, up.pos - 2); push(makeArithCmd(pop(), lineStr(up.pos))) ~~> = "((" :whitespace(Whitespace) ArList "))" ;
ArithCmdDyn <~~ arSqClaim(up.start + 2, up.pos - 2); badClaim(up.start + 2, up.pos - 2, true); push(makeArithCmd(mkArDyn(pop()), lineStr(up.pos))) ~~> = "((" :whitespace() ArHasExp ArDynWord "))" ;
// A text the arithmetic grammar rejects (`(( 1 + ))`) is still an arithmetic command: the
// run-time evaluator reports bash's syntax error and the command fails with status 1.
ArithCmdRaw <~~ arSqClaim(up.start + 2, up.pos - 2); badClaim(up.start + 2, up.pos - 2, true); push(makeArithCmd(mkArDyn(pop()), lineStr(up.pos))) ~~> = "((" :whitespace() ArDynWord "))" ;
// [[ ... ]] : the conditional command (no word splitting, glob and regex matching).
CondCmd     <~~ push(makeCondCmd(pop(), lineStr(up.pos))) ~~> = "[[" :whitespace(Whitespace) CondOr Linebreak "]]" ;

RedirList   = RedirItem { RedirItem } ;
RedirItem   = HereDoc | Redir ;

// let EXPR ... : the same evaluator as (( )); a quoted argument is parsed as arithmetic.
// Redirections may stand among the arguments (`let x=1 2>/dev/null`).
LetCmd      <~~ push(makeLetCmd(takeAll(), lineStr(up.start))) ~~> = KwLet LetItem { LetItem } ;
KwLet       = "let" KwEnd ;
LetItem     = RedirItem | NotIoRedir LetArg ;
// Each argument is a WORD: expanded, field-split where it is unquoted, and every field is
// then one expression. e='1 + 2': `let "x = $e * 2"` sets 5, `let x=$e*2` is the three
// arguments x=1 + 2*2 (x=1, then a syntax error). A word with no expansion is parsed here
// instead, unless the next word would read as the rest of its expression (`let c=1 + d=2`,
// `let x=0 || y`): LetIsWord decides.
LetArg      = LetWord | LetDq | LetSq | LetAr | LetRaw ;
LetAr       <~~ arClaim(up.start, up.pos); push(pop()) ~~> = ArAssign ;
LetWord     <~~ push({letws: takeAll()}) ~~> = LetIsWord CWord ;
LetDq       <~~ arClaim(up.start + 1, up.pos - 1); push(pop()) ~~> = '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ArList '"' ;
LetSq       <~~ arClaim(up.start + 1, up.pos - 1); push(pop()) ~~> = "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ArList "'" ;
// A word that is no expression at all (`let "3+"`) is evaluated at run time, which reports it.
LetRaw      <~~ push({letws: takeAll()}) ~~> = CWord ;

// local / declare / typeset / export / readonly [-flags] NAME[=WORD] ...
// Spelled as its own production (rather than an ordinary command with word arguments)
// because the declared NAME has to be known statically: the compiler needs it to pick a
// variable slot, and `local` needs it to record the save entry for the current call frame.
// It is not a SimpleCommand, so it needs its own [ RedirList ] for
// `declare -p missing 2>/dev/null` to parse.
DeclCommand <~~ push(makeDeclR(takeAll(), lineStr(up.start))) ~~>
            = DeclKw { DeclFlag } { DeclItem } [ RedirList ] ;
DeclKw      <~~ push({dkw: up.in}) ~~> = DeclWord KwEnd ;
DeclWord    = "local" | "declare" | "typeset" | "export" | "readonly" ;
DeclFlag    <~~ push({dflag: up.in}) ~~> = DeclSign :whitespace() DeclFlagCh { DeclFlagCh } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// -X gives an attribute, +X takes it away.
DeclSign    = "-" | "+" ;
DeclFlagCh  = "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" ;
DeclItem    = DeclAssign | DeclArr | DeclName | DeclDyn ;
DeclAssign  <~~ push(mkDeclItem(takeAll(), arSrc(up.start, up.pos))) ~~>
            = AsgIdent <~~ push({aname: up.in}) ~~> AsgTail ;
DeclName    <~~ push({dname: up.in}) ~~> = Name KwEnd ;
// NAME[SUB] without a value declares NAME an array, the subscript never evaluated (bash 5.3:
// `declare c[1/0]` is status 0 and `declare -p c` prints `declare -a c`); {darr} is the
// operand as written. An empty SUB is no operand ("not a valid identifier").
DeclArr     <~~ push({dname: up.in.slice(0, up.in.indexOf("[")), darr: up.in}) ~~>
            = AsgIdent :whitespace() "[" !@+" \t\r\n;&|<>()[]'\"\\$`" "]" :whitespace(Whitespace) KwEnd ;
// Any other word is an operand known only once it is expanded (`local "$1"`,
// `declare $v='x y'`, `typeset s${dyn}+=foo`, `declare 'q=1'`): its fields are read as
// NAME, NAME=VALUE or an option when it runs. {dsrc} is the word as written (bashDeclLate).
DeclDyn     <~~ push({dyn: takeAll(), dsrc: arSrc(up.start, up.pos)}) ~~> = NotIoRedir CWord ;

// name=value : the = must stick to the name (no surrounding spaces), so the disable
// (:whitespace()) sits in THIS sequence, right after the identifier. The value is optional
// (name= is valid) and starts immediately after = with no space.
// It may carry redirections, before, among or after (`r=$(cmd) 2>/dev/null`): bash performs
// them after the assignment, so they do not apply to a command substitution on the right hand side.
// Several may share one command (`a=1 b=$a`): they run left to right, each seeing the last.
Assignment  <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll(), "" + lnPop())) ~~> = [ RedirList ] AsgOne1 { AsgOne | RedirItem } ;
AsgOne1     <~~ lnPush(up.pos) ~~> = AsgOne ;
AsgOne      <~~ push({a: parseAssign(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = AsgIdent <~~ push({aname: up.in}) ~~> AsgTail ;

// The right hand side of an assignment: a word, or a parenthesised array literal. `+=`
// appends (a string for a scalar, elements for an array).
AsgTail     = [ AsgIdx ] :whitespace() AsgOp2 :whitespace() AsgRhs ;
// {aidx} is the subscript as written, which bash's "NAME[SUB]: bad array subscript" quotes.
// The subscript of an indexed array is arithmetic text in which a quoted part keeps its
// quotes, a $'...' one quoted as by bash's sh_single_quote, and an escape its backslash
// (bash 5.3: `a[$'1']=5` and `a[\1]=5` are arithmetic syntax errors), while the key of an
// associative one is the word: mkAsgIdx makes both.
AsgIdx      <~~ push(mkAsgIdx(takeAll(), arSrc(up.start + 1, up.pos - 1))) ~~> = "[" :whitespace() { AsgIdxSeg } "]" ;
AsgIdxSeg   = AsgIdxQ | SubXSeg ;
AsgIdxQ     <~~ push(idxQuoted(pop(), arSrc(up.start, up.pos))) ~~> = AnsiSeg | SqSeg | EscSeg ;
AsgOp2      <~~ push({aop2: up.in}) ~~> = "+=" | AsgEq ;
AsgRhs      = ArrayLit | AsgWord ;
AsgWord     = [ NsWord ] ;
// THE RIGHT HAND SIDE OF AN ASSIGNMENT IS NOT FIELD-SPLIT. `y=$x` with
// x="  a   b  " assigns the value CHARACTER FOR CHARACTER; only the bare words of an
// array literal - `A=($x)` - are split, and they go through CWord below. NsWord is the
// same segment list read in the mode a double-quoted word uses: "$@" and "${a[@]}" still
// contribute several fields, which is what makes `c=$@` join them with a space.
NsWord      <~~ badClaim(up.start, up.pos, true); push(mkNsWord(takeAll())) ~~>
            = WordFirst :whitespace() { WordSeg } ;
ArrayLit    <~~ push(mkArrayLit(takeAll())) ~~> = "(" :whitespace(WsNL) ArrayItems ")" ;
ArrayItems  = { ArrayItem } ;
ArrayItem   = ArrayKV | ArrayWord ;
// The element as written ({src}) is what an empty associative key reports.
ArrayWord   <~~ push({aw: pop(), src: arSrc(up.start, up.pos)}) ~~> = CWord ;
// `[k]=v` inside an array literal is an assignment too: bash 5.3 keeps `A=([0]=$x)`
// unsplit exactly as it keeps `A=$x`, and `[k]=` stores the empty string.
ArrayKV     <~~ push(mkArrayKV(takeAll(), arSrc(up.start, up.pos))) ~~> = "[" :whitespace() IdxWord "]" :whitespace() "=" :whitespace() [ NsWord ] ;
WsNL        = { @+" \t\r\n" | Comment } ;
AsgIdent    = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) :whitespace() { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" } ;

// A command word followed by argument words. NotReserved blocks a bare reserved keyword in
// command position (so 'done' / 'fi' / ... end an enclosing list instead of being commands).
SimpleCommand <~~ push(makeSimpleCommand(takeAll(), "" + lnPop(), null)) ~~>
            = NotReserved FirstWord { CmdSuffix } ;
FirstWord   <~~ lnPush(up.pos) ~~> = CWord ;
CmdSuffix   = HereDoc | Redir | CWord ;
// Redirections BEFORE the command word (`2>/dev/null cmd`), or with no command at all: bash
// 5.3 performs `>file` alone (status 0) and `>&6` with fd 6 closed fails with status 1.
// Without a command word this is `:` with the redirections.
RedirCommand <~~ push(makeSimpleCommand(redirCmdItems(takeAll()), "" + lnPop(), null)) ~~>
            = RedirFirst { RedirItem } [ NotReserved CWord { CmdSuffix } ] ;
RedirFirst  <~~ lnPush(up.pos) ~~> = RedirItem ;

// A here-document. Its body lives on the lines after the command line, so it cannot be
// described by ordinary productions: BsSplice moves each body next to its marker and HdScan
// (both in lib/bash-splice.abnf) reads the two as ONE token, which mkHereDoc splits apart
// again. <<< is a here-string, which RedirTok handles instead.
HereDoc     <~~ push(mkHereDoc(up.in, up.pos)) ~~> = HdScan :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// The body of an unquoted here-document, re-parsed with this grammar. mec -verify reports
// HereBodyW and its parts as unreachable, which is correct and expected: nothing in the start
// rule refers to them, they are entered through c.parseFrom from mkHereDoc.
// Literal runs, escapes
// and $ expansions, exactly like the inside of a double-quoted string. The leading "\x01" is
// prepended by mkHereDoc so the production can start with a token and then turn whitespace
// skipping off (a production may not START with a Command - that is read as a repetition).
HereBodyW   <~~ badClaim(up.start + 1, up.pos, true); push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = "\x01" :whitespace() HereSegs ;
HereSegs    = { HereSeg } ;
HereSeg     = DqExpSeg | DqEsc | HereLit ;
HereLit     <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"$\\`" ;

// Redirections. `<` declines a second "<" so a here-document HdScan refused cannot fall
// through and be mis-read as two input redirections.
Redir       <~~ push(mkRedir(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ IoNum ] RedirOp [ Word ] ;
// An fd number touches its operator: in `echo 1 >&2` the 1 is an argument, not fd 1.
IoNum       <~~ push({iofd: up.in}) ~~> = "0"..."9" :whitespace() { "0"..."9" } RedirNext ;
RedirOp     <~~ push({redir: up.in}) ~~> = RedirTok ;
RedirTok    = "<<<" | "&>>" | "&>" | ">>" | ">&" | ">|" | "<&" | ">" | LtOne ;
LtOne       = "<" NotLt ;


// --- words: a run of segments with NO whitespace between them ---------------------
// The first segment is matched under the caller's whitespace state (so leading space is
// skipped in argument position, but NOT after = in an assignment); :whitespace() then turns
// skipping off for the remaining segments so foo\"bar\"$x concatenates as one word.

Word        <~~ badClaim(up.start, up.pos, true); push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~>
            = WordFirst :whitespace() { WordSeg } ;
WordFirst   = Tilde | WordSeg ;
WordSeg     = SqSeg | LocSeg | DqSeg | AnsiSeg | UqExp | EscSeg | BraceLit | EgPfxLit | BareSeg ;

// An expansion OUTSIDE quotes is subject to word splitting on IFS; the same production
// inside a double-quoted string (or a ${...} word) is not, which is the only difference
// between the two - so the split-able form is marked here rather than in the expansion.
UqExp       <~~ push(mkSplittable(pop())) ~~> = ExpSeg ;

// A word in COMMAND ARGUMENT position, where brace expansion applies and one written word
// can therefore become several. Real bash does not brace-expand an assignment's right hand
// side (a=x{1,2} really is the four characters x{1,2}), which is why this is its own rule.
CWord       <~~ badClaim(up.start, up.pos, true); pushWords(mkWords(takeAll())) ~~>
            = CWordFirst :whitespace() { CWordSeg } ;
CWordFirst  = Tilde | CWordSeg ;
CWordSeg    = SqSeg | LocSeg | DqSeg | AnsiSeg | UqExp | EscSeg | BraceRange | BraceAlts | BraceLit | EgPfxLit | BareSeg ;

// {a,b,c} needs at least one comma; {x} is literal. {1..5} {5..1} {a..e} {1..9..3} are ranges.
BraceAlts   <~~ push(mkBraceAlts(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" :whitespace() BraceAlt "," BraceAlt { "," BraceAlt } "}" ;
BraceAlt    <~~ push({alt: takeAll()}) ~~> = { BraceAltSeg } ;
BraceAltSeg = SqSeg | LocSeg | DqSeg | AnsiSeg | ExpSeg | EscSeg | BraceRange | BraceAlts | AltBare ;
AltBare     <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+" \t\n\r;$'\"\\(){},&|<>`" ;
BraceRange  <~~ push(mkBraceRange(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" :whitespace() RangeEnd ".." RangeEnd [ RangeStep ] "}" ;
RangeEnd    <~~ push({rend: up.in}) ~~> = RangeTok ;
RangeStep   <~~ push({rstep: up.in}) ~~> = ".." RangeNum ;
RangeTok    = RangeNum | RangeAlpha ;
RangeNum    = [ "-" ] "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" } ;
RangeAlpha  = "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" ;
BraceLit    <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = "{" | "}" ;

// ~ or ~/... at the START of a word expands to $HOME.
Tilde       <~~ push(mkTilde()) ~~> = "~" TildeEnd ;

// $'...' : ANSI-C quoting. The body is taken raw and decoded by ansiC (\n \t \xHH \0NNN \uHHHH ...).
AnsiSeg     = "$'" :whitespace() AnsiBody "'" ;
AnsiBody    <~~ push(makeConst(ansiC(up.in, true))) ~~> = { AnsiEsc | AnsiLit } ;
AnsiEsc     = "\\" ( AnyCh | "\n" ) ;
AnsiLit     = !@+"'\\" ;

// 'single quoted' : everything up to the next ' is literal (no escapes, may span lines).
SqSeg       = "'" :whitespace() { !@+"'" } <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> "'" ;

// "double quoted" : literal runs, escapes and $ expansions concatenated.
DqSeg       <~~ badClaim(up.start, up.pos, false); push(mkDqWord(takeAll())) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { DqExpSeg | DqEsc | DqLit } '"' ;
// $"..." : a string translated by the locale's message catalog, which in the C locale has
// none, so it is the double-quoted string (bash 5.3: echo $"a b"c prints a bc).
LocSeg      <~~ badClaim(up.start, up.pos, false); push(mkDqWord(takeAll())) ~~>
            = '$"' :whitespace() { DqExpSeg | DqEsc | DqLit } '"' ;
DqLit       <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~>
            = !@+"\"$\\`" ;
DqEsc       <~~ push(makeConst(dqEsc(up.in))) ~~>
            = "\\" AnyCh ;

// An unquoted \x escape: the backslash is dropped, the next char is literal (\  -> space).
// No blank is skipped after the backslash, also where the escape begins a word.
EscSeg      <~~ push(makeConst(escChar(up.in))) ~~>
            = "\\" :whitespace() AnyCh ;

// The extended globs @(a|b) ?(a|b) *(a|b) +(a|b) !(a|b). Only PatSeg - the right hand
// side of == / != inside [[ ]] - refers to ExtGlob, on purpose: there bash needs no shopt,
// while elsewhere a `shopt -s extglob` in the same file does not make a later line parse
// (bash parses before it runs), so accepting extglobs in ordinary words would accept
// scripts bash rejects. They are ordinary PATTERN text as
// far as the word is concerned - the group is rebuilt literally (with $ expansions and
// quoted arms substituted) and globAt() is what understands it. Nesting is allowed:
//     [[ --verbose=1 == --@(help|verbose=@(1|2)) ]]   is true in bash.
ExtGlob     = ExtGlobPfx EgOpen EgSegs EgClose ;
ExtGlobPfx  <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = EgPfxTok EgLParen ;
EgPfxTok    = "@" | "?" | "*" | "+" | "!" ;
// BareSeg's greedy run must not swallow the "@" of "--@(help|verbose)", so the five extglob
// prefix characters are excluded from it and matched by this segment instead whenever they
// are NOT opening a group (ordered choice puts ExtGlob first). A word is the concatenation
// of its segments, so "a@b" is still exactly "a@b" - it is just three segments now.
EgPfxLit    <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = EgPfxTok ;
EgOpen      <~~ push(makeConst("(")) ~~> = "(" :whitespace() ;
EgClose     <~~ push(makeConst(")")) ~~> = ")" ;
EgSegs      = { EgSeg } ;
EgSeg       = SqSeg | DqSeg | ExpSeg | EscSeg | ExtGlob | EgBar | EgPfxLit | EgBare ;
EgBar       <~~ push(makeConst("|")) ~~> = "|" ;
EgBare      <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"|()\"'\\$`@?*+!" ;

// A bare run: any char that is not whitespace, a separator, or a shell metacharacter.
// "#" is NOT excluded: it only opens a comment at the START of a word, and a word can never
// start with one here because the Whitespace rule (which contains Comment) has already run
// before the first segment. So `echo a#b` prints a#b and `[[ 64#a -eq 10 ]]` reads the
// base-N literal, as in bash.
BareSeg     <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~>
            = !@+" \t\n\r;$'\"\\(){}&|<>`@?*+!" ;

AnyCh       = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;


// --- $ expansions -----------------------------------------------------------------

ExpSeg      = ArithExp | CmdSub | BackSub | FunSub | ReplySub | DollarBrace | DollarName | DollarDigit | DollarSpecial | DollarLit ;

// $( LIST ) and the older `LIST` : run the list with stdout captured, and the value of the
// word segment is that output with its trailing newlines removed (exactly like the shell).
// The list is parsed with the normal whitespace rule; the enclosing word's "no skipping"
// state is restored automatically when the production returns.
CmdSub      <~~ push(mkCmdSub(pop(), bashCsLines(arSrc(up.start + 2, up.pos - 1), srcLine(up.start)))) ~~>
            = "$(" :whitespace(Whitespace) CompoundList ")" ;
// Inside `...` a backslash before $, ` or \ (and before " when the `...` stands in double
// quotes) is removed first, and the remaining text is then parsed as the list (POSIX 2.6.3):
// "`echo \\ `" runs `echo \ `. mkBackBody parses it when it first runs.
BackSub     <~~ push(mkCmdSub(mkBackBody(arSrc(up.start + 1, up.pos - 1), false), {first: srcLine(up.start), skips: []})) ~~>
            = "`" :whitespace() BqText "`" ;
DqBackSub   <~~ push(mkCmdSub(mkBackBody(arSrc(up.start + 1, up.pos - 1), true), {first: srcLine(up.start), skips: []})) ~~>
            = "`" :whitespace() BqText "`" ;
BqText      = { "\\" ( AnyCh | "\n" ) | !@+"`\\" } ;
// bash 5.3's function substitutions run the list in the CURRENT shell, so what it assigns
// stays: `${ LIST; }` (a blank after the brace) is its captured output, trailing newlines
// removed; `${| LIST; }` is the value LIST leaves in REPLY, which is local to it.
FunSub      <~~ push(mkFunSub(pop(), false)) ~~>
            = "${" :whitespace() FunSubBlank :whitespace(Whitespace) CompoundList "}" ;
FunSubBlank = @+" \t\n" ;
ReplySub    <~~ push(mkFunSub(pop(), true)) ~~>
            = "${|" :whitespace(Whitespace) CompoundList "}" ;

// $(( expr )) : whitespace IS allowed inside; the value is the decimal string of the result.
// bash EXPANDS the expression's text first and only then parses it, so an expansion can
// contribute operators: e="1 + 2"; $(( $e * 2 )) is 5, not 6. A text holding an expansion (or
// a double quote, which is removed) is therefore kept as a word and read by the run-time
// evaluator, arEval; a text without one is parsed here, once.
ArithExp    = ArithDyn | ArithSt | ArithRaw | ArithBrk ;
ArithSt     <~~ arClaim(up.start + 3, up.pos - 2); push(mkArith(pop())) ~~> = "$((" :whitespace(Whitespace) ArList "))" ;
ArithDyn    <~~ arSqClaim(up.start + 3, up.pos - 2); badClaim(up.start + 3, up.pos - 2, true); push(mkArith(mkArDyn(pop()))) ~~> = "$((" :whitespace() ArHasExp ArDynWord "))" ;
ArithRaw    <~~ arSqClaim(up.start + 3, up.pos - 2); badClaim(up.start + 3, up.pos - 2, true); push(mkArith(mkArDyn(pop()))) ~~> = "$((" :whitespace() ArDynWord "))" ;
ArDynWord   <~~ push(mkNsWord(takeAll())) ~~> = { ArDynSeg } ;
ArDynSeg    = LocSeg | DqSeg | ArDynAnsi | ExpSeg | ArDynParen | ArDynSub | ArDynBare | ArDynLb ;
// A $'...' part is its value single-quoted (sh_single_quote), which arithmetic refuses:
// bash 5.3 reports $(( a[$'1'] )) at '1'.
ArDynAnsi   <~~ push(makeConst(bashSq(ansiC(arSrc(up.start + 2, up.pos - 1), true)))) ~~> = "$'" :whitespace() { AnsiEsc | AnsiLit } "'" ;
ArDynParen  = ArDynOpen { ArDynSeg } ArDynClose ;
ArDynOpen   <~~ push(makeConst("(")) ~~> = "(" ;
ArDynClose  <~~ push(makeConst(")")) ~~> = ")" ;
// In a subscript a single-quoted part (and a $'...' one) is its text, unexpanded, when the
// arithmetic text holds an expansion, and stays quoted when it does not (arSqClaim):
// bash 5.3, with x=1, $(( a['1'] + $x )) is a[1]+1, $(( a['$x'] )) an error at "$x" and
// $(( a['1'] )) one at "'1'".
ArDynSub    = ArDynSubOpen { ArDynSubSeg } ArDynSubClose ;
ArDynSubOpen <~~ push(makeConst("[")) ~~> = "[" ;
ArDynSubClose <~~ push(makeConst("]")) ~~> = "]" ;
ArDynSubSeg = ArDynSq | ArDynSubDq | ArDynSubAnsi | ArDynSubExp | ArDynParen | ArDynSub | ArDynSubBare ;
ArDynSubDq  <~~ push(arSubProt(up.start, pop())) ~~> = LocSeg | DqSeg ;
ArDynSubExp <~~ push(arSubProt(up.start, pop())) ~~> = ExpSeg ;
ArDynSq     <~~ push(arSqPart(up.start, up.in, arSrc(up.start + 1, up.pos - 1))) ~~> = "'" { !@+"'" } "'" ;
ArDynSubAnsi <~~ push(arSqAnsi(up.start, ansiC(arSrc(up.start + 2, up.pos - 1), true))) ~~> = "$'" :whitespace() { AnsiEsc | AnsiLit } "'" ;
ArDynSubBare <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"()[]$`\"'" ;
ArDynBare   <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"()[$`\"" ;
// A "[" without its "]".
ArDynLb     <~~ push(makeConst("[")) ~~> = "[" ;
// $[ expr ] : the obsolete spelling of $(( expr )), which bash still expands. Its text ends at
// the "]" that closes the "$[", so a subscript's brackets are matched inside it.
ArithBrk    = ArithBrkDyn | ArithBrkSt | ArithBrkRaw ;
ArithBrkSt  <~~ arClaim(up.start + 2, up.pos - 1); push(mkArith(pop())) ~~> = "$[" :whitespace(Whitespace) ArList "]" ;
ArithBrkDyn <~~ arSqClaim(up.start + 2, up.pos - 1); badClaim(up.start + 2, up.pos - 1, true); push(mkArith(mkArDyn(pop()))) ~~> = "$[" :whitespace() ArHasExp ArBrkWord "]" ;
ArithBrkRaw <~~ arSqClaim(up.start + 2, up.pos - 1); badClaim(up.start + 2, up.pos - 1, true); push(mkArith(mkArDyn(pop()))) ~~> = "$[" :whitespace() ArBrkWord "]" ;
ArBrkWord   <~~ push(mkNsWord(takeAll())) ~~> = { ArBrkSeg } ;
ArBrkSeg    = LocSeg | DqSeg | ArDynAnsi | ExpSeg | ArDynParen | ArDynSub | ArBrkBare ;
ArBrkBare   <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"()[]$`\"" ;

// ${...} : the whole parameter-expansion family. Inside the braces whitespace is NOT
// skipped (a default word may contain spaces), and the enclosing state is restored on return.
DollarBrace <~~ push(mkParamExp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "${" :whitespace() PeBody "}" ;
// "#" (length) and "!" (indirection) are PREFIXES of a whole parameter expansion, not just of
// a name: bash parses `${#x-default}` and `${!ref-default}` and `${!r@a}`. It refuses the
// first at RUN time ("bad substitution", {badsub}) and honours the other two, which is what
// mkParamExp does with the {lenp} / {indp} markers. Separate marker keys, not {pe}: the
// inner form pushes {pe} of its own and `${###}` needs both.
// Ordered choice COMMITS: once an alternative matches, a later failure of the "}" kills the
// whole ${...}, it does not retry the next alternative. So the two alternatives that can end
// early - the bare length form and the bare name - assert the closing brace themselves with
// PeBraceEnd, which is what lets `${##}` (length of $#, = 1) and `${###}` ($# with an empty
// "#" strip, = 2) both come out the way bash prints them.
PeBody      = PeLen | PeIndForm | PeCore | PeLenBad | PeBad ;
PeLen       <~~ push({lenp: 1}) ~~> = "#" PeTarget PeBraceEnd ;
// `${#x-default}` and `${#a[0]/1/xxx}` PARSE in bash and then fail at run time with
// "bad substitution"; this is the alternative that reaches that documented behaviour.
PeLenBad    <~~ push(badRec(up.start, arSrc(up.start, up.pos), false)) ~~> = "#" PeCore ;
// Any other text between the braces parses as well and is the same run-time error
// (`${(m)foo}`, a zsh idiom): the message, and the command line is abandoned with status 1.
// Not after a blank or "|", which open a function substitution (FunSub) in bash, so one
// without its closing "}" is the syntax error it is there.
PeBad       <~~ push(badRec(up.start, arSrc(up.start, up.pos), false)) ~~> = PeBadFirst { PeBadCh } ;
PeBadFirst  = !@+"} \t\n|" ;
PeBadCh     = !@+"}" ;
PeIndForm   <~~ push({indp: 1}) ~~> = "!" ( PePfxNames | PeCore ) ;
PeCore      = PePatForm | PeCaseForm | PeReplForm | PeXform | PeOpForm | PeSubstrForm | PePlain ;
PeOpForm    = PeTarget PeOp [ BraceWord ] ;
PePlain     = PeTarget PeBraceEnd ;
// ${!prefix*} / ${!prefix@} : the NAMES of every set variable starting with that prefix.
// PeBraceEnd, or `${!r@a}` would match this rule with PfxTok = "@" and then choke on the
// "a}" it left behind - ordered choice commits, so it would never reach PeCore's PeXform.
PePfxNames  <~~ push({pfx: up.in}) ~~> = PeName PfxTok PeBraceEnd ;
PfxTok      = "*" | "@" ;
// ${v@Q} ${v@U} ${v@L} ${v@u} ${v@E} ${v@a} ${v@A} ${v@K} ${v@k} ${v@P} : the transform
// operators. Verified against GNU bash 5.3.15 with x='a b' and a=(pa pb):
//     @Q -> 'a b'   @U -> A B   @L -> a b   @u -> A b   @E -> a b   @P -> a b
//     @A -> x='a b', with a `declare -FLAGS ` prefix when the name has an attribute
//     @a -> the attribute letters (i for `declare -i n`, ai for `declare -ai x`)
// With a @ or * SUBSCRIPT, four of them stop being element-wise and describe the whole
// variable instead:
//     ${a[@]@A} -> the three fields  declare  -a  a=([0]="pa" [1]="pb")
//     ${a[@]@a} -> `a a`, one copy per element      ${a[@]@K} -> 0 "pa" 1 "pb"
//     ${a[@]@k} -> the four fields  0 pa 1 pb
// and @Q/@K/@k/@A of an UNSET name are EMPTY, where a set-but-empty one gives ''.
PeXform     = PeTarget "@" PeXfOp ;
PeXfOp      <~~ push({xf: up.in}) ~~> = "Q" | "E" | "P" | "A" | "K" | "a" | "k" | "L" | "U" | "u" ;

// NAME or NAME[SUBSCRIPT] - the subscript is a word, read as an arithmetic index for an
// indexed array and as a literal key for an associative one. "@" and "*" mean the whole array.
// An empty SUBSCRIPT is no target: `${b[]}` is the run-time "bad substitution" (PeBad).
// peTgt: the target as written, the prefix of an arithmetic error in ${v:off} (ArOff).
PeTarget    <~~ peTgt = arSrc(up.start, up.pos) ~~> = PeIdxRef | PeName ;
// An indexed array's SUB is arithmetic text that keeps a quoted part's quotes, as an
// assignment's does (AsgIdx): bash 5.3 answers ${a['1']} and $(( a[\1] )) with an
// arithmetic syntax error. A $'...' part is re-quoted outside double quotes and is its
// value inside them ("${a[$'1']}" is a[1]). SubIdx pushes the word, then the {aiw, aqw,
// aqd} record PeIdxRef, ArLhsElem and ArElem take back.
PeIdxRef    <~~ push(peIdxItem(pop(), arSrc(up.start, up.pos))) ~~> = PeName "[" !"]" SubIdx "]" ;
SubIdx      <~~ pushSubIdx(mkAsgIdx(takeAll(), "")) ~~> = :whitespace() { AsgIdxSeg } ;
IdxWord     <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = { IdxSeg } ;
IdxSeg      = AnsiSeg | SubXSeg ;
// The subscript of a NAME[SUB] string a builtin was handed (subExpand): no $'...' there.
SubXWord    <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = { SubXSeg } ;
SubXSeg     = SqSeg | DqSeg | ExpSeg | EscSeg | IdxPair | IdxBare ;
// A subscript holds brackets in pairs, so one may nest: ${a[j[0]]}, a[j[1]]=x.
IdxPair     = IdxOpen { IdxSeg } IdxClose ;
IdxOpen     <~~ push(makeConst("[")) ~~> = "[" ;
IdxClose    <~~ push(makeConst("]")) ~~> = "]" ;
// The same subscript of an indexed array: expanded as in double quotes, where a ' is a
// character and a " is removed (subExpandDq).
SubDqWord   <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = SubDqSeg :whitespace() { SubDqSeg } ;
SubDqSeg    = DqExpSeg | DqEsc | SubDqQuote | SubDqLit ;
SubDqQuote  <~~ push(makeConst("")) ~~> = '"' ;
SubDqLit    <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"\"$\\`" ;
IdxBare     <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"[]}\"'\\$`" ;
PeName      <~~ push({pname: up.in}) ~~> = PeNameTok ;
PeNameTok   = PeIdent | PeDigits | "@" | "*" | "#" | "?" | "!" | "$" | "-" ;
PeIdent     = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" } ;
PeDigits    = "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" } ;
// Longest first: ":-" must be tried before "-" (see the ordered-choice rule).
PeOp        <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = ":-" | ":=" | ":?" | ":+" | "-" | "=" | "?" | "+" ;
// ${v#pat} ${v##pat} ${v%pat} ${v%%pat} : strip a matching prefix / suffix (# from the
// front, % from the back; doubled = longest match).
PePatForm   = PeTarget PePatOp PeGlobArg ;
PePatOp     <~~ push({pe: up.in}) ~~> = "##" | "#" | "%%" | "%" ;
// The PATTERN operand of ${v#p} ${v%p} ${v^p} and ${v/p/r}: a quoted part is literal, as in
// [[ == ]] (`${s%"$p"}` with p='?' strips nothing).
PeGlobArg   <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = { PeGlobSeg } ;
PeGlobSeg   = PatSq | PatDq | PatAnsi | ExpSeg | PatEsc | BraceBare ;

// ${v^} ${v^^} ${v,} ${v,,} : case conversion of the first / of every character.
PeCaseForm  = PeTarget PeCaseOp [ PeGlobArg ] ;
PeCaseOp    <~~ push({pe: up.in}) ~~> = "^^" | "^" | ",," | "," | "\x7e\x7e" | "\x7e" ;

// ${v/pat/rep} first match, ${v//pat/rep} every match, ${v/#pat/rep} anchored at the
// start, ${v/%pat/rep} anchored at the end. The replacement may be omitted (delete).
// Only a single "/" takes an anchor: after "//" a "#" or "%" is pattern text, so
// `v=aab; echo ${v//#a/x}` is aab in bash 5.3.
PeReplForm  = PeTarget ( PeReplAll | PeReplOne [ PeReplAnchor ] ) ReplPat [ ReplTail ] ;
PeReplAll   <~~ push({pe: up.in}) ~~> = "//" ;
PeReplOne   <~~ push({pe: up.in}) ~~> = "/" ;
PeReplAnchor <~~ push({anchor: up.in}) ~~> = "#" | "%" ;
ReplPat     <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = { ReplSeg } ;
ReplSeg     = PatSq | PatDq | PatAnsi | ExpSeg | PatEsc | ReplBare ;
ReplBare    <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"}/\"'\\$`" ;
// The REPLACEMENT: an unquoted "&" is the matched text (bash 5.2+'s patsub_replacement), so
// a quoted or backslash-escaped part has its "&" and "\" escaped (repEscape), which the
// replace step undoes (repSubst / rt_repsub).
ReplTail    = "/" ReplArg ;
ReplArg     <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = { ReplArgSeg } ;
ReplArgSeg  = RepSq | RepDq | RepAnsi | ExpSeg | RepEsc | BraceBare ;
RepSq       = "'" :whitespace() { !@+"'" } <~~ push(makeConst(repEscape(up.in))) ~~> "'" ;
RepDq       <~~ push(mkQuotedRep(mkDqWord(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { DqExpSeg | DqEsc | DqLit } '"' ;
RepEsc      <~~ push(makeConst(repEscape(escChar(up.in)))) ~~> = "\\" AnyCh ;
RepAnsi     = "$'" :whitespace() RepAnsiBody "'" ;
RepAnsiBody <~~ push(makeConst(repEscape(ansiC(up.in, true)))) ~~> = { AnsiEsc | AnsiLit } ;

// ${v:off} and ${v:off:len} : the offsets are arithmetic, so whitespace matters again -
// which is also what tells ${v: -1} (a substring) from ${v:-default}.
// An omitted offset or length is legal and means 0 / "to the end":
//     $ x=abc;   echo "[${x::1}]"      ->  [a]
//     $ a=(1 2); echo "${a[@]: }"      ->  1 2
// PeArPad: whitespace skipping is OFF again for DollarBrace's own closing "}", so a blank
// between the last arithmetic token and the brace (`${foo:3 :-3 }`, `${a[@]: }`) has to be
// eaten while this production still has the Whitespace rule installed.
PeSubstrForm <~~ push({pe: "substr"}) ~~> = PeTarget ":" :whitespace(Whitespace) ArOff [ PeSubLen ] :whitespace() PeArPad ;
PeSubLen    = ":" ArOff ;
ArOff       <~~ arClaim(up.start, arPastWs(up.pos)); badClaim(arWsBefore(up.start), arPastWs(up.pos), true); push(arIn(peTgt + ": ", pop())) ~~> = ArList ArEndHere | ArEmpty | ArOffDyn ;
// Any other offset is read by the run-time evaluator, as $(( )) reads one (ArithRaw): a
// single quote is no quote there (bash 5.3: ${a[@]:'1'} is an arithmetic syntax error).
ArOffDyn    <~~ push(mkArDyn(pop())) ~~> = ArOffWord ;
ArOffWord   <~~ push(mkNsWord(takeAll())) ~~> = ArOffSeg { ArOffSeg } ;
ArOffSeg    = LocSeg | DqSeg | ArDynAnsi | ExpSeg | ArDynParen | ArOffBare ;
ArOffBare   <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"()$`\":}" ;
ArEmpty     <~~ push(mkNum("0")) ~~> = ArEndHere ;
PeArPad     = { PeBlank } ;
PeBlank     = @+" \t" ;
// Inside "..." a $'...' or $"..." part of an offset or a length is its text, as if written
// there (bash's extquote, on by default): bash 5.3 prints "${s:$'1'}" and "${s:$"1"}" as ello
// where the unquoted ${s:$'1'} is an arithmetic syntax error.
DqSubstrForm <~~ push({pe: "substr"}) ~~> = PeTarget ":" :whitespace(Whitespace) DqArOff [ DqSubLen ] :whitespace() PeArPad ;
DqSubLen    = ":" DqArOff ;
DqArOff     <~~ arClaim(up.start, arPastWs(up.pos)); badClaim(arWsBefore(up.start), arPastWs(up.pos), true); push(arIn(peTgt + ": ", pop())) ~~> = ArList ArEndHere | ArEmpty | DqArOffDyn ;
DqArOffDyn  <~~ push(mkArDyn(pop())) ~~> = DqArOffWord ;
DqArOffWord <~~ push(mkNsWord(takeAll())) ~~> = DqArOffSeg { DqArOffSeg } ;
DqArOffSeg  = DqSeg | DqXqAnsi | LocSeg | ExpSeg | ArDynParen | ArOffBare ;
DqXqAnsi    = "$'" :whitespace() DqXqAnsiB "'" ;
DqXqAnsiB   <~~ push(makeConst(ansiC(up.in, true))) ~~> = { AnsiEsc | AnsiLit } ;
// An offset that is a $'...' whose text starts with -, =, ? or + is read again as that text
// written there: bash 5.3, s=hello, prints "${s:$'-1'}" as hello (the ${s:-1} default form)
// and "${s:$'+1'}" as 1.
DqAnsiOpForm = PeTarget DqAnsiOp DqAnsiOpWord ;
DqAnsiOp    <~~ push({op: ":" + up.in.slice(up.in.length - 1)}) ~~> = ":$'" ( "-" | "=" | "?" | "+" ) ;
DqAnsiOpWord <~~ push(mkBraceWord(takeAll())) ~~> = DqXqAnsiB "'" { DqBrSeg } ;

// A word inside ${...}: anything up to the closing brace, expansions included. A $'...'
// part is ANSI-C quoted there too, even inside "...": bash 5.3 prints `"${u:-$'\x41'}"` as A.
BraceWord   <~~ push(mkBraceWord(takeAll())) ~~> = BraceWSeg { BraceWSeg } ;
BraceWSeg   = SqSeg | LocSeg | DqSeg | AnsiSeg | BraceUq | EscSeg ;
// The UNQUOTED parts of a ${v-w} word: split when the ${...} itself stands unquoted
// (see mkBraceWord).
BraceUq     <~~ push({uq: pop()}) ~~> = ExpSeg | BraceBare ;
BraceSeg    = SqSeg | LocSeg | DqSeg | AnsiSeg | ExpSeg | EscSeg | BraceBare ;
BraceBare   <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"}\"'\\$`" ;

// The expansions of a double-quoted context ("...", an unquoted here-document body). They
// differ only in the word of ${v-w} ${v=w} ${v+w} ${v?w} (colon forms too): there a ' is an
// ordinary character that is KEPT, though it still hides a "}" from the brace match, and the
// text between two of them is expanded. Backslash follows the double-quote rule, plus \}.
// bash 5.3, u unset:  "${u:-'a}b'}" -> 'a}b'   "${u:-'$x'}" -> 'abc'   "${u:-\'}" -> \'
DqExpSeg    = ArithExp | CmdSub | DqBackSub | FunSub | ReplySub | DqDollarBrace | DollarName | DollarDigit | DollarSpecial | DollarLit ;
DqDollarBrace <~~ push(mkParamExp(dqIdxItems(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = "${" :whitespace() DqPeBody "}" ;
DqPeBody    = PeLen | DqPeIndForm | DqPeCore | PeLenBad | DqPeBad ;
// In double quotes, a bad ${NAME[...} whose "[" is never closed is reported with the whole
// word it is in (badRec, badClaim): bash 5.3, echo x"${m[[]-v}"y is "bad substitution: no
// closing `}' in x"${m[[]-v}"y".
DqPeBad     <~~ push(badRec(up.start, arSrc(up.start, up.pos), true)) ~~> = PeBadFirst { PeBadCh } ;
DqPeIndForm <~~ push({indp: 1}) ~~> = "!" ( PePfxNames | DqPeCore ) ;
DqPeCore    = PePatForm | PeCaseForm | PeReplForm | PeXform | DqPeOpForm | DqAnsiOpForm | DqSubstrForm | PePlain ;
DqPeOpForm  = PeTarget PeOp [ DqBraceWord ] ;
DqBraceWord <~~ push(mkBraceWord(takeAll())) ~~> = DqBrSeg { DqBrSeg } ;
DqBrSeg     = DqBrSq | DqBrDq | AnsiSeg | LocSeg | DqBrGlue | DqExpSeg | DqBrEsc | BraceBare ;
// Between the two 's a " is still a quote character and is removed:
// "${u:-'a"b'}" -> 'ab'   "${u:-'a"}"b'}" -> 'a}b'
DqBrSq      = DqBrQuote { GlueRef | DqExpSeg | DqBrEsc | DqBrSqDq | DqBrSqLit } DqBrQuote ;
// The quotes of this word are removed BEFORE it is expanded, so a $NAME runs on across
// them. bash 5.3, x=abc xb=ZZ:  "${u:-a"$x"b}" -> aZZ   "${u:-'$x"b'}" -> 'ZZ'
// "${u:-"\}"}" -> }   while "${u:-a"${x}"b}" -> aabcb. GlueRef reads such a name; when
// it crossed an odd number of quotes (GlueFlip) the parse continues on the other side of
// an inner "..." (DqBrDqRest is the inside, up to its closing quote).
DqBrDq      <~~ push(mkDqWord(takeAll())) ~~> = '"' :whitespace() DqBrDqRest ;
DqBrDqRest  = '"' | GlueRef GlueFlip | ( GlueRef | DqExpSeg | DqBrEsc | DqLit ) DqBrDqRest ;
DqBrGlue    = GlueRef [ DqBrGlueDq ] ;
DqBrGlueDq  <~~ push(mkDqWord(takeAll())) ~~> = GlueFlip DqBrDqRest ;
GlueRef     = "$" :whitespace() GlueName ;
GlueName    <~~ push(mkVarRef(up.in.split('"').join(""))) ~~>
            = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) { GlueNc | GlueQ } ;
GlueNc      = "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" ;
GlueQ       = '"' { '"' } GlueNc ;
DqBrSqDq    = '"' ;
DqBrQuote   <~~ push(makeConst("'")) ~~> = "'" ;
DqBrSqLit   <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"'\"$\\`" ;
DqBrEsc     <~~ push(makeConst(up.in == "\\}" ? "}" : dqEsc(up.in))) ~~> = "\\" AnyCh ;
DollarName  = "$" VarName <~~ push(mkVarRef(up.in)) ~~> ;
DollarDigit = "$" ArgDigit <~~ push(mkVarRef(up.in)) ~~> ;
DollarSpecial = DollarHash | DollarQ | DollarStar | DollarAt | DollarPid | DollarBang | DollarDash ;
DollarHash  <~~ push(mkVarRef("#")) ~~> = "$#" ;
DollarQ     <~~ push(mkVarRef("?")) ~~> = "$?" ;
DollarStar  <~~ push(mkVarRef("*")) ~~> = "$*" ;
DollarAt    <~~ push(mkVarRef("@")) ~~> = "$@" ;
DollarPid   <~~ push(mkVarRef("$")) ~~> = "$$" ;
DollarBang  <~~ push(mkVarRef("!")) ~~> = "$!" ;
DollarDash  <~~ push(mkVarRef("-")) ~~> = "$-" ;
DollarLit   <~~ push(makeConst("$")) ~~> = "$" NotDollarBrace ;

VarName     = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) :whitespace() { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" } ;
ArgDigit    = "0"..."9" ;


// --- $(( )) arithmetic: the full bash operator set ---------------------------------
// Precedence, loosest first: , then assignment, ?:, ||, &&, |, ^, &, == !=, comparison,
// shifts, + -, * / %, ** (right associative), unary, primary.

ArList      <~~ push(foldComma(takeAll())) ~~> = ArAssign { "," ArAssign } ;
ArAssign    = ArAssignForm | ArCond ;
ArAssignForm <~~ push(mkArAssign(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~> = ArLhs ArAsgOp ArAssign ;
// NAME[SUBSCRIPT] is an array element, an operand and an assignment target alike:
// `$(( nums[0] + nums[2] ))`, `(( m[p]++ ))`, `(( a[i] += 2 ))`. An empty SUBSCRIPT leaves
// the expression to the run-time evaluator, which has bash's errors for it (arSubscript).
ArLhs       =ArLhsElem | ArLhsName ;
ArLhsElem   <~~ push(mkArLhs(takeAll())) ~~> = ArElemName "[" !"]" SubIdx "]" ;
ArElemName  <~~ push({lhs: up.in}) ~~> = ArName ;
ArLhsName   <~~ push({lhs: up.in}) ~~> = ArName ;
ArElem      <~~ push(mkArElem(takeAll())) ~~> = ArElemName "[" !"]" SubIdx "]" ;
ArAsgOp     <~~ push({aop: up.in}) ~~>
            = "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | "<<=" | ">>=" | "&=" | "^=" | "|=" | ArPlainAsg ;
ArPlainAsg  = "=" NotEq ;

ArCond      <~~ push(mkArCond(takeAll())) ~~> = ArLor [ "?" ArAssign ":" ArAssign ] ;
ArLor       <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArLand { ArLorOp ArLand } ;
ArLorOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "||" ;
ArLand      <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArBor { ArLandOp ArBor } ;
ArLandOp    <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "&&" ;
ArBor       <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArBxor { ArBorOp ArBxor } ;
ArBorOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "|" NotBar ;
ArBxor      <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArBand { ArBxorOp ArBand } ;
ArBxorOp    <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "^" NotEq ;
ArBand      <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArEq { ArBandOp ArEq } ;
ArBandOp    <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "&" NotAmp ;
ArEq        <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArCmp { ArEqOp ArCmp } ;
ArEqOp      <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "==" | "!=" ;
ArCmp       <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArShift { ArCmpOp ArShift } ;
ArCmpOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ;
ArShift     <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArAdd { ArShiftOp ArAdd } ;
ArShiftOp   <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "<<" | ">>" ;
ArAdd       <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArMul { ArAddOp ArMul } ;
ArAddOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "+" NotPlus | "-" NotMinus ;
ArMul       <~~ push(foldBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ArPow { ArMulOp ArPow } ;
ArMulOp     <~~ push({op: up.in, at: up.pos}) ~~> = "*" NotStar | "/" | "%" ;
ArPow       <~~ push(foldPow(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~> = ArUnary [ ArPowOp ArPow ] ;
ArPowOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = "**" ;

ArUnary     = ArPreInc | ArNeg | ArPos | ArNot | ArBnot | ArPrimary ;
ArPreInc    <~~ push(mkIncDec(takeAll())) ~~> = ArIncOp ArLhs ;
ArIncOp     <~~ push({iop: up.in, pre: 1}) ~~> = "++" | "--" ;
ArNeg       <~~ push(mkUn("-", pop())) ~~> = "-" ArUnary ;
ArPos       <~~ push(mkUn("+", pop())) ~~> = "+" ArUnary ;
ArNot       <~~ push(mkUn("!", pop())) ~~> = "!" NotEq ArUnary ;
ArBnot      <~~ push(mkUn("~", pop())) ~~> = "~" ArUnary ;

// A $-prefixed operand (Bash expands it before evaluating): $name, $1..$9, $# and $?.
ArPrimary   = ArGroup | ArBaseDyn | ArNum | ArPostInc | ArArithSub | ArCmdSub | ArDollarDigit | ArDollarHash | ArDollarQ | ArDollarVar | ArDollarBrace | ArElem | ArVar ;
ArGroup     = "(" ArList ")" ;
ArPostInc   <~~ push(mkIncDec(takeAll())) ~~> = ArLhs ArPostOp ;
ArPostOp    <~~ push({iop: up.in, pre: 0}) ~~> = "++" | "--" ;
ArNum       <~~ push(mkNum(up.in)) ~~> = ArNumTok :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ArNumTok    = ArHex | ArBase | ArDec ;
ArHex       = ArHexPfx :whitespace() ArHexDig { ArHexDig } ;
ArHexPfx    = "0x" | "0X" ;
ArHexDig    = "0"..."9" | "a"..."f" | "A"..."F" ;
ArBase      = ArDec :whitespace() "#" ArAlnum { ArAlnum } ;
// BASE#DIGITS WHERE EITHER SIDE IS BUILT BY EXPANSION. Real bash expands the whole
// arithmetic string first and only then lexes it, so `10#$num` is `10#0012` is 12; here the
// expression is a parse tree, so the two operands are parsed as WORDS (literal characters
// plus any $ expansion) and joined at run time.
//
// The BASE side admits only DIGITS as literal text, which is what keeps this rule from
// swallowing anything else: bash rejects a bare name there too - `b=16; echo $(( b#ff ))` is
// "invalid arithmetic operator", while `$(( $b#ff ))` is 255 - so `b#ff` must still fail
// here and reach ArVar exactly as before. It has to be tried BEFORE ArNum: ArNumTok's own
// ArBase alternative fails on `10#$num` and ArDec then matches just "10", and PEG ordered
// choice never comes back (manual chapter 4).
//
// WHITESPACE: a LEADING `:whitespace()` on this production makes the rule never match, so
// it sits AFTER the first element, like ArHex's `ArHexPfx :whitespace() ArHexDig`. The two
// operand productions turn skipping off after their first segment so `$(( 1 6#3 ))` stays
// an error instead of reading "1" "6" across the blank as base 16.
ArBaseDyn   <~~ push(mkArBaseDyn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ArBaseLhs :whitespace() "#" ArBaseRhs :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ArBaseLhs   <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = ArBaseLSeg :whitespace() { ArBaseLSeg } ;
ArBaseLSeg  = ExpSeg | ArBaseDig ;
ArBaseDig   <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = "0"..."9" ;
ArBaseRhs   <~~ push(mkWord(takeAll())) ~~> = ArBaseRSeg :whitespace() { ArBaseRSeg } ;
ArBaseRSeg  = ExpSeg | ArBaseAl ;
ArBaseAl    <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = ArAlnum ;
ArAlnum     = "0"..."9" | "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "@" | "_" ;
ArDec       = "0"..."9" :whitespace() { "0"..."9" } ;
ArVar       <~~ push(mkArVar(up.in)) ~~> = ArName ;
ArDollarVar = "$" ArName <~~ push(mkArVar(up.in)) ~~> ;
ArDollarBrace <~~ push(mkArDyn(pop())) ~~> = DollarBrace :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A nested $(( )) is arithmetic, not a $( ( ) ) command substitution, so it is tried first.
ArArithSub  <~~ push(mkArDyn(pop())) ~~> = ArithExp :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ArDollarDigit = "$" ArgDigit <~~ push(mkArVar(up.in)) ~~> :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ArDollarHash <~~ push(mkArVar("#")) ~~> = "$#" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ArDollarQ   <~~ push(mkArVar("?")) ~~> = "$?" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ArName      = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) :whitespace() { "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// $( LIST ) inside $(( )) : the captured output is read as an integer.
ArCmdSub    <~~ push(mkArCmdSub(pop())) ~~> = CmdSub :whitespace(Whitespace) ;


// --- [[ ... ]] conditional expressions ----------------------------------------------

CondOr      <~~ push(foldCondOr(takeAll())) ~~> = CondAnd { Linebreak "||" CondAnd } ;
CondAnd     <~~ push(foldCondAnd(takeAll())) ~~> = CondNot { Linebreak "&&" CondNot } ;
// A newline may stand before a term, around && and ||, and before ) and ]] (bash's
// cond_skip_newlines), but never between the words of one term.
CondNot     = Linebreak CondTerm ;
CondTerm    = CondNeg | CondPrim ;
CondNeg     <~~ push(mkCondNot(pop())) ~~> = "!" CondNot ;
CondPrim    = CondParen | CondUn | CondMatch | CondBin | CondSingle ;

// [[ STR =~ ERE ]] : the right hand side is a REGEX, so ( ) | + ? { } are ordinary
// characters there and need a word production of their own. Quoted parts stay literal and
// $ expansions still happen, exactly as in bash.
CondMatch   <~~ push(mkCondMatch(takeAll())) ~~> = NsWord CondMatchOp RegexWord ;
CondMatchOp <~~ push({cop: up.in}) ~~> = "=~" ;
RegexWord   <~~ push(mkNsWord(takeAll())) ~~> = RegexSeg :whitespace() { RegexSeg } ;
// A QUOTED or backslash-escaped part of the regex is literal ([[ abc =~ "a.c" ]] is false
// in bash 5.3), so those segments are regex-escaped.
RegexSeg    = RxSq | RxDq | ExpSeg | RxEsc | RxGroup | RegexBare ;
RxSq        = "'" :whitespace() { !@+"'" } <~~ push(makeConst(regexEscape(up.in))) ~~> "'" ;
RxDq        <~~ push(mkQuotedRx(mkDqWord(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { DqExpSeg | DqEsc | DqLit } '"' ;
RxEsc       <~~ push(makeConst(regexEscape(escChar(up.in)))) ~~> = "\\" :whitespace() AnyCh ;
RegexBare   <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+" \t\n\r;&<>$'\"\\`(" ;
// Inside ( ) blanks and ; & < > are ordinary characters as well: bash 5.3 reads
// [[ $s =~ ^([^ ]+)(a;b) ]] as one regex word.
RxGroup     = RxOpen :whitespace() { RxSq | RxDq | ExpSeg | RxEsc | RxGroup | RxInBare } RxClose ;
RxOpen      <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = "(" ;
RxClose     <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = ")" ;
RxInBare    <~~ push(makeConst(up.in)) ~~> = !@+"()\n$'\"\\`" ;
CondParen   = "(" CondOr Linebreak ")" ;
CondUn      <~~ push(mkCondUn(takeAll())) ~~> = CondUnOp NsWord ;
CondUnOp    <~~ push({cop: up.in}) ~~> = CondUnTok KwEnd ;
CondUnTok   = "-z" | "-n" | "-v" | "-e" | "-f" | "-d" | "-s" | "-r" | "-w" | "-x" | "-h" | "-L" | "-b" | "-c" | "-p" | "-S" | "-t" | "-o" | "-R" | "-a" | "-g" | "-u" | "-k" | "-O" | "-G" | "-N" ;
// The right hand side of == / != inside [[ ]] is a PATTERN, and a quoted part of it is
// literal (`[[ $s == "h*" ]]` does not glob), so quoted segments are glob-escaped here.
CondBin     <~~ push(mkCondBin(takeAll())) ~~> = NsWord CondBinOp PatWord ;
PatWord     <~~ badClaim(up.start, up.pos, true); push(mkNsWord(takeAll())) ~~> = PatSeg :whitespace() { PatSeg } ;
PatSeg      = PatSq | PatDq | PatAnsi | ExpSeg | PatEsc | ExtGlob | EgPfxLit | BareSeg ;
PatSq       = "'" :whitespace() { !@+"'" } <~~ push(makeConst(globEscape(up.in))) ~~> "'" ;
PatDq       <~~ badClaim(up.start, up.pos, false); push(mkQuotedPat(mkDqWord(takeAll()))) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { DqExpSeg | DqEsc | DqLit } '"' ;
PatAnsi     = "$'" :whitespace() PatAnsiBody "'" ;
PatAnsiBody <~~ push(makeConst(globEscape(ansiC(up.in, true)))) ~~> = { AnsiEsc | AnsiLit } ;
PatEsc      <~~ push(makeConst(globEscape(escChar(up.in)))) ~~> = "\\" :whitespace() AnyCh ;
CondBinOp   <~~ push({cop: up.in}) ~~> = CondBinTok ;
CondBinTok  = "==" | "!=" | "-eq" | "-ne" | "-lt" | "-le" | "-gt" | "-ge" | "-nt" | "-ot" | "-ef" | "=" | "<" | ">" ;
// A lone word is not followed by a newline: bash reads the next token as a binary operator.
CondSingle  <~~ push(mkCondSingle(pop())) ~~> = NsWord !"\n" ;


// --- keywords, keyword boundary and the reserved-word guard ------------------------

KwIf        = "if" KwEnd ;
KwThen      = "then" KwEnd ;
KwElif      = "elif" KwEnd ;
KwElse      = "else" KwEnd ;
KwFi        = "fi" KwEnd ;
KwFor       = "for" KwEnd ;
KwWhile     = "while" KwEnd ;
KwDo        = "do" KwEnd ;
KwDone      = "done" KwEnd ;
KwIn        = "in" KwEnd ;
KwCase      = "case" KwEnd ;
KwUntil     = "until" KwEnd ;
KwFunction  = "function" KwEnd ;
KwEsac      = "esac" KwEnd ;

// Fails when the next thing (after any run of blanks and newlines) is a clause terminator,
// so a case body's separator run stops in front of ";;" / ";&" / ";;&".
// "|" that is not the front of "||", and "<" that is not the front of "<<" - both anchored
// to the very next byte, which a !"literal" lookahead cannot do (it skips whitespace first).
NotBar = :script(~~
    (function() {
        if (c.peek(0) === 124) { return abnf.newToken("\x01bar", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;
// Fails before an IO-numbered redirection (`2>`, `10<`): after `a=x` that is the
// assignment's redirection, not a command word "2".
NotIoRedir = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
        var d = i
        while (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
        if (i > d && (cc === 60 || cc === 62)) { return abnf.newToken("\x01io-redir", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;
NotLt = :script(~~
    (function() {
        if (c.peek(0) === 60) { return abnf.newToken("\x01lt", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;
// Succeeds right after a GlueRef that crossed an odd number of quotes: counts the " back
// to its $.
GlueFlip = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = -1
        var q = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc !== 36 && cc >= 0) {
            if (cc === 34) { q = q + 1 }
            i = i - 1
            cc = c.peek(i)
        }
        if (q % 2 === 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01even", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;

// A tilde only expands when the word ends there or continues with "/".
TildeEnd = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var cc = c.peek(0)
        if (cc == -1 || cc == 47) { return undefined }
        if (cc == 32 || cc == 9 || cc == 10 || cc == 13) { return undefined }
        if (cc == 59 || cc == 38 || cc == 124 || cc == 41 || cc == 62 || cc == 60) { return undefined }
