    //
    // What the last name gets is not "the remaining fields joined": `IFS=: read -r a b <<<
    // "p::q:r:"` gives b=":q:r:" and `read -r a b <<< "  x   y   z  "` gives b="y   z". The
    // rule, checked against bash 5.3.15:
    //
    //   - EXACTLY ONE field left: that field, delimiters and all whitespace gone.
    //     `read -r a <<< "  only  "` is "only"; `IFS=: read -r a b <<< "p:q:"` is "q".
    //   - TWO OR MORE: the RAW text from the start of that field to the end of the record,
    //     less trailing IFS WHITESPACE only, quoted or not. `IFS=: read -r a <<< "p:q:"`
    //     keeps its trailing colon; `IFS=: read -r a b <<< ":a:b:"` is "a:b:"; and an IFS
    //     that does not contain the space leaves a trailing space alone ("a::: " -> ":: ").
